00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:36.670
for some questions and some
answers that are coming.

00:33:36.670 --> 00:33:37.960
We're collecting them right now.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:39.310
We'll be back in a few
minutes, and we'll answer

00:33:39.310 --> 00:33:40.180
those questions for you guys.

00:33:40.180 --> 00:33:40.900
So stay tuned.

00:36:05.430 --> 00:36:06.220
Welcome back.

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:07.800
We have a few great
questions from you guys.

00:36:07.800 --> 00:36:09.258
We're going to go
through them now.

00:36:09.258 --> 00:36:11.610
And hopefully, everybody
will be satisfied at the end.

00:36:11.610 --> 00:36:13.380
So the first question
is, "Where do I

00:36:13.380 --> 00:36:15.360
go to build my own
data connection?

00:36:15.360 --> 00:36:17.100
How long does it
take to do that?"

00:36:17.100 --> 00:36:19.590
So the developer
documentation is available

00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:23.100
at developers.googl
e.com/datastudio.

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:24.780
What you're looking
for is a feature

00:36:24.780 --> 00:36:26.340
called Community Connectors.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:28.740
And the documentation goes
through how to actually build

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:31.100
a connector, how it
works from end to end,

00:36:31.100 --> 00:36:32.070
and how to test them.

00:36:32.070 --> 00:36:33.640
And it provides some samples.

00:36:33.640 --> 00:36:35.730
We also have a GitHub
repository that gives you

00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:38.700
some really great detailed
examples of how this code works

00:36:38.700 --> 00:36:39.880
and how you can build them.

00:36:39.880 --> 00:36:41.800
How long does it
take to do that?

00:36:41.800 --> 00:36:43.926
So that really depends on
the complexity of the API

00:36:43.926 --> 00:36:46.341
you're connecting to, how well
you know the service you're

00:36:46.341 --> 00:36:47.100
connecting to.

00:36:47.100 --> 00:36:49.349
A lot of the work, at least
in my personal experience,

00:36:49.349 --> 00:36:51.360
is that when you're
working with a new API,

00:36:51.360 --> 00:36:52.200
that's what takes a long time.

00:36:52.200 --> 00:36:53.491
It's just learning the new API.

00:36:53.491 --> 00:36:54.900
How does their service work?

00:36:54.900 --> 00:36:57.327
The actual writing of the
code for the connector

00:36:57.327 --> 00:36:58.410
is fairly straightforward.

00:36:58.410 --> 00:37:00.720
And I'd probably expect you
would be getting something

00:37:00.720 --> 00:37:04.320
up and running within probably
an hour, at the very least.

00:37:04.320 --> 00:37:06.940
And then probably what's going
to take more time, over time,

00:37:06.940 --> 00:37:09.480
is just really finalizing
that and making

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:11.520
sure it's working
well and testing it,

00:37:11.520 --> 00:37:13.737
if you intend to provide
it to others to use.

00:37:13.737 --> 00:37:15.820
It's really more around
the quality control there.

00:37:15.820 --> 00:37:18.330
So yeah, fairly quickly--
probably like 10, 15 minutes,

00:37:18.330 --> 00:37:19.260
if you're really--

00:37:19.260 --> 00:37:20.180
if you're familiar
with app scripts,

00:37:20.180 --> 00:37:21.210
you'll get something working.

00:37:21.210 --> 00:37:22.880
So it's actually quite
easy and cool to play with.

00:37:22.880 --> 00:37:23.600
MINHAZ KAZI: Yep.

00:37:23.600 --> 00:37:27.360
The second question is, "What
kinds of new fields or metrics

00:37:27.360 --> 00:37:29.070
can I create in Data Studio?"

00:37:29.070 --> 00:37:30.510
So Data Studio
provides a feature

00:37:30.510 --> 00:37:32.310
where you can create
calculated metrics.

00:37:32.310 --> 00:37:34.632
And we have more than
70 different functions

00:37:34.632 --> 00:37:35.340
that you can use.

00:37:38.061 --> 00:37:39.810
Depending on what data
you're bringing in,

00:37:39.810 --> 00:37:43.560
you can add different
fields together.

00:37:43.560 --> 00:37:47.310
You can do analytical
functions on them.

00:37:47.310 --> 00:37:49.050
If you go to the
support center, there's

00:37:49.050 --> 00:37:50.600
the whole list of
all the functions

00:37:50.600 --> 00:37:52.650
available and how
you can use them.

00:37:52.650 --> 00:37:55.440
You can also do some analytical
functions, like aggregations,

00:37:55.440 --> 00:37:58.170
percentages of total,
and things like those.

00:38:00.389 --> 00:38:01.930
The third question
is, "You mentioned

00:38:01.930 --> 00:38:03.360
public data sets on BigQuery.

00:38:03.360 --> 00:38:06.330
Can you show how to connect
to them from Data Studio?"

00:38:06.330 --> 00:38:08.490
So on BigQuery, there are
multiple public data sets

00:38:08.490 --> 00:38:11.400
available for anyone to use.

00:38:11.400 --> 00:38:13.350
To use those, what
you'll have to do

00:38:13.350 --> 00:38:17.040
is basically go into BigQuery
and come up with any query

00:38:17.040 --> 00:38:19.020
that you might want to do.

00:38:19.020 --> 00:38:21.660
You can query the whole
table, and then you

00:38:21.660 --> 00:38:23.510
can go into Data Studio--

00:38:23.510 --> 00:38:26.180
we can show that.

00:38:26.180 --> 00:38:27.905
Well, if you go to the support--

00:38:27.905 --> 00:38:29.410
OK, we can actually show that.

00:38:29.410 --> 00:38:31.076
PETE FRISELLA: Yeah,
we'll just shot it.

00:38:31.076 --> 00:38:36.480
MINHAZ KAZI: So let's
go into Data Studio,

00:38:36.480 --> 00:38:38.505
and we'll try to
add a new report.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.010
We'll create a new data source.

00:38:44.010 --> 00:38:45.112
We'll add BigQuery.

00:38:45.112 --> 00:38:47.070
PETE FRISELLA: So this
is a BigQuery connector.

00:38:47.070 --> 00:38:47.350
MINHAZ KAZI: Right.

00:38:47.350 --> 00:38:48.330
PETE FRISELLA: It's going to
show you public data sets.

00:38:48.330 --> 00:38:50.081
MINHAZ KAZI: So there's
a public data set.

00:38:50.081 --> 00:38:51.788
You'll have to pick
your billing project.

00:38:51.788 --> 00:38:53.490
You can get the
free billing project

00:38:53.490 --> 00:38:57.600
that lets you use the
data within some quota.

00:38:57.600 --> 00:39:00.990
You can pick any public
data set you want.

00:39:00.990 --> 00:39:04.890
Let's say, we'll pick the
World Bank Health population.

00:39:04.890 --> 00:39:07.380
And you can pick which
table you want to look into.

00:39:07.380 --> 00:39:08.970
And then if you connect,
that will connect to BigQuery

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:09.910
and let you use the data.

00:39:09.910 --> 00:39:10.725
PETE FRISELLA: Yeah.

00:39:10.725 --> 00:39:12.210
And also, we encourage you
to go to the Help Center

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:12.900
documentation.

00:39:12.900 --> 00:39:14.850
This will also explain how
to do this process, right?

00:39:14.850 --> 00:39:15.558
MINHAZ KAZI: Yes.

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:19.160
PETE FRISELLA: And
the next question

00:39:19.160 --> 00:39:21.470
is, "Data Studio is free.

00:39:21.470 --> 00:39:22.130
That's great."

00:39:22.130 --> 00:39:23.240
Yes, it is.

00:39:23.240 --> 00:39:25.370
"If I upload my own
data, where does it go?

00:39:25.370 --> 00:39:26.810
Is that storage also free?"

00:39:26.810 --> 00:39:29.330
So in most cases, the connection
is actually going directly

00:39:29.330 --> 00:39:30.170
to the end service.

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:32.030
So in that case, we're not
actually storing anything.

00:39:32.030 --> 00:39:34.650
There is some level of caching,
just for performance reasons.

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:36.920
But we actually don't store
anything in that case.

00:39:36.920 --> 00:39:39.530
There are some connectors,
like the CSV upload,

00:39:39.530 --> 00:39:40.430
or the file upload.

00:39:40.430 --> 00:39:43.007
In that case, yes, then we would
be storing that actual data.

00:39:43.007 --> 00:39:44.090
And there are some limits.

00:39:44.090 --> 00:39:45.250
I think there--

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:47.180
MINHAZ KAZI: It's a 2
gigabytes per person.

00:39:47.180 --> 00:39:49.740
And if you upload
your own CSV file,

00:39:49.740 --> 00:39:51.250
that'll get stored
on Cloud storage.

00:39:51.250 --> 00:39:51.740
PETE FRISELLA: Cloud storage.

00:39:51.740 --> 00:39:52.240
Yeah.

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:54.990
Again, if you go to the
Help Center documentation,

00:39:54.990 --> 00:39:57.180
around the file
upload, CSV upload,

00:39:57.180 --> 00:39:58.760
it will discuss
what the limits are

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:00.070
and where that data's stored.

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:01.820
Otherwise, typically,
it's coming directly

00:40:01.820 --> 00:40:02.540
from the service.

00:40:02.540 --> 00:40:04.430
MINHAZ KAZI: Right.

00:40:04.430 --> 00:40:05.990
So the last question
we have is "What

00:40:05.990 --> 00:40:07.880
about non-image URLs in data?

